**K-02.** Zones are tenant-defined. Separate zone sets per factor (heating value, pressure, rate), each with effective dates and assigned to the location or meter.

**K-04.** Neutral internal terms ("sales service" and "transportation service") with a per-jurisdiction label table, and the tenant supplies the exact tariff wording at setup. The pipeline safety fee is an effective-dated rule with amount, basis (per customer or per service line), billing timing, citation and optional recovery-report deadline. Research notes for Texas: a 2009 law raised the per-service-line cap to one dollar, but the current Utilities Code §121.211 text limits total fees to program cost and the Commission sets the amount by rule (16 TAC §8.201), so there is no single fixed cap. One 2024 tariff shows $1.22 per customer, and another requires a recovery report within 90 days after the last billing cycle in which the fee is billed. Confirm the current amount against §8.201 before loading it.

## Wave 7 decisions

**R-11.** Estimation, late fees and program exclusions all become dated rule data with a legal citation.

**K-06.**
- No early-pay discounts, because gas is regulated. Drop that rule type.
- The meter test fee is outcome-driven, with tolerance and fee as rule data, confirmed with the tenant at setup.
- Rounding goes to the cent, and rates and intermediate math carry four decimals (0.0000). Kyle confirmed 2026-10-15: standard rounding to the nearest cent, which can go either up or down (half-up at the midpoint).
- Accessible notice formats: a customer preference field (large print, braille, language) and a record of the format actually sent.

**K-07.** Support municipal utilities with rules entered per utility and no assumed defaults. Not required at launch.

